{"id":"F551","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"This only checks the robustness of F528, and it is bounded by size filters. GEO counts research deposits, which lag sequencing by a lot (see the derived item) and are dated by release. They say little about the current clinical 6000 tail, which is the 1A issue. The mouse/human Crnic series is a side note."},
{"id":"F552","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"The spike series carry 2021-era accession numbers (GSE169xxx, GSE181xxx). They are old data released late, not new sequencing. That confirms the spikes are artifacts, but it also shows that GEO PDAT does not measure current 6000 use. n_samples counts all platforms, so the run-rate of ~21–25K excluding them is approximate. The direction for 1A is ambiguous, because steady 6000 deposits fit management's 'stays' view as well as ours."},
{"id":"F558","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1B","direction":"supports","note":"Checked on the page. The per-run figures are 'as low as' prices, with the page saying it transfers 'the price of the instrument on top of reagents'. The MiSeq ($868) and iSeq ($227) figures are below kit prices, so the figure is an uplift on top of reagents, not an all-in price. The $520K/yr and the ~$1.56M vs $1.49M lease cross-check hold. Correction: the effect applies only to rental boxes (~5 of 100+ Q4'25 placements, known), so the fleet-level inflation of X pull-through is small (see the derived item), not ~40%. Relabeled 1B because it bears on how much true X reagent price decline is hidden. The page is undated."},
{"id":"F559","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1B","direction":"supports","note":"This is a new source type (a job posting), but it restates known management statements on application-specific pricing. That it 'includes the 35B' is an inference. Pay-for-performance can also protect price, so the direction is not certain."},
{"id":"F561","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1B","direction":"supports","note":"Relabeled to 1B (competition). IVDR Class A is self-declared and a low bar. AVITI is a mid-throughput box, so it is not a like-for-like replacement for 6000Dx high-throughput sites. Being 'available in select countries in late 2026' is a plan, not a shipment."},
{"id":"F562","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1B","direction":"against","note":"This is a single snapshot of a job board, and Element may sell VITARI through its existing reps. It is a weak signal."},
{"id":"F566","verdict":"reject","strength":"weak","label":"1B","direction":"supports","note":"Misread. The $110–112M Q2 guide is TOTAL revenue. The same slide guides exome/genome revenue at '~$100 million' on ~30,000 tests (~$3,333/test). Actual ES/GS was ~$100.3M on 30,785 tests ($3,258), so ES/GS revenue met guide and revenue per test was only ~2% below the implied guide, not 12%. The FY cut ($540–555M to $475–490M) is already priced into the Q2 guide. This is also the customer's reimbursement, not Illumina's price."},
{"id":"F567","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1B","direction":"supports","note":"The arithmetic is consistent with the guide: ES/GS volume 'at least 30%' and revenue 'at least 20%' give about −7.7% per test. This is customer-side reimbursement pressure. It links to Illumina discounting only indirectly, through the customer's push to cut reagent costs."},
{"id":"F568","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1B","direction":"supports","note":"Best of the GeneDx items. Management says genome costs about 2x an exome, 'almost entirely higher reagent costs', and expects reagent prices to fall, with cost per test flat in H2'26 and step-downs in 2027. That is a large customer planning on supplier price cuts. F239 (no validated alternative platform) limits its leverage, so the effect is pressure rather than a credible switch."},
{"id":"F575","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1A","direction":"against","note":"These are list prices from the Open Offer oncology appendix. Many clinical 6000 users run LDTs on RUO kits, and the 6000Dx base is small, so the size of the effect is limited. It still undercuts the idea that the IVD-locked tail is the highest-priced Gb. It also means converting regulated sites costs less revenue per Gb than the blended 3.4x implies (see the derived item)."},
{"id":"F577","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"The sums tie (Q3'26 6000 57,557 = 36,312 human + 21,245 mouse). The big caveat: GEO puts the X at only 29.5% of 6000+X samples in Q3'26, while Illumina reports research Gb at 92% on the X. GEO therefore lags actual sequencing by about 2+ years and reflects research publication, not the current clinical tail. Use it only as a lagged directional indicator."},
